===== STATUS: success =====
Question: Can large language models solve university mathematics and computer science course questions?

Yes, large language models demonstrate substantial capability in solving university-level mathematics and computer science course questions. GPT-3.5 successfully solves approximately one-third of MIT's entire Mathematics and EECS curriculum, while GPT-4 with prompt engineering achieves perfect performance on non-image-based questions from the same coursework (zhang2023exploringthemit pages 1-3).

Multiple prompting techniques enhance LLM performance on course-level problems. These include few-shot learning, chain-of-thought reasoning, and novel approaches such as expert prompting (zhang2023exploringthemit pages 1-3). Additionally, fine-tuning open-source models on curated datasets improves their performance on reasoning tasks (zhang2023exploringthemit pages 1-3).

Beyond static evaluation, LLMs can further improve through iterative self-reflection mechanisms. The Reflexion framework demonstrates that language agents achieve 91% pass@1 accuracy on programming benchmarks (HumanEval) and successfully solve knowledge-intensive reasoning questions through trial-and-error with reflective feedback (shinn2023reflexionlanguageagents pages 1-2). This suggests LLMs possess capability for complex problem-solving characteristic of university-level coursework.

These findings collectively indicate that state-of-the-art LLMs can solve significant portions of university-level mathematics and computer science problems, with performance varying by model architecture, prompting strategy, and question modality.

